<h2 id=intro>Introduction</h2>

*This section is non-normative.*

Many pages show a list of records: rows in a table, items in a list, options in a menu. When the
records live in a JSON file, authors today write script that fetches the file, copies a
<{template}> element once for each record, and fills in the copies. The script is similar on every
page, and nothing appears when script is turned off or fails to load.

This specification lets authors declare the same thing in markup. The <{template/json}> attribute
tells a template where its data is, the <{html-global/template}> attribute tells an element which
template to render into itself, and the <{html-global/key}> attribute marks the elements in the
template that receive data.

<div class=example id=example-intro-table>
    The file <code>./json/tableData.json</code> contains:

    <xmp highlight=json>
    [
      { "city": "Seattle", "state": "Washington" },
      { "city": "Portland", "state": "Oregon" },
      { "city": "Boise", "state": "Idaho" }
    ]
    </xmp>

    The page contains:

    <xmp highlight=html>
    <template id="tableRow" json="./json/tableData.json">
      <tr><td key="city"></td><td key="state"></td></tr>
    </template>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>City</th><th>State</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody template="tableRow">
        <tr><td colspan="2">Loading…</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </xmp>

    When the file loads, the user agent replaces the contents of the <{tbody}> element with three
    rows. In each row, the <code>td</code> whose key is <code>city</code> holds that item's
    <code>city</code>, and the one whose key is <code>state</code> holds its <code>state</code>:

    <xmp highlight=html>
    <tbody template="tableRow">
      <tr><td key="city">Seattle</td><td key="state">Washington</td></tr>
      <tr><td key="city">Portland</td><td key="state">Oregon</td></tr>
      <tr><td key="city">Boise</td><td key="state">Idaho</td></tr>
    </tbody>
    </xmp>
</div>

<div class=example id=example-intro-attributes>
    A property whose value is an object sets attributes too. Its <code>content</code> property
    becomes the element's text, and every other property sets the attribute with that name.

    <xmp highlight=json>
    [
      {
        "city":  "Seattle",
        "link":  { "href": "/wa/seattle", "content": "Details" },
        "photo": { "src": "seattle.jpg", "alt": "Seattle skyline" }
      }
    ]
    </xmp>

    <xmp highlight=html>
    <template id="cityRow" json="./json/cities.json">
      <tr>
        <td key="city"></td>
        <td><a key="link"></a></td>
        <td><img key="photo"></td>
      </tr>
    </template>

    <table>
      <tbody template="cityRow"></tbody>
    </table>
    </xmp>

    renders:

    <xmp highlight=html>
    <tbody template="cityRow">
      <tr>
        <td key="city">Seattle</td>
        <td><a key="link" href="/wa/seattle">Details</a></td>
        <td><img key="photo" src="seattle.jpg" alt="Seattle skyline"></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
    </xmp>
</div>

User agents that don't support this specification ignore all three attributes and keep showing the
element's original contents, here the "Loading…" row. Authors can put a useful fallback there, such
as a link to the data.

Note: In HTML syntax, a <code>/</code> at the end of a start tag has no effect on elements that
aren't void. <code>&lt;td key="city"/></code> is an ordinary start tag, and
<code>&lt;tbody template="tableRow"/></code> doesn't end the <{tbody}> element. The examples in this
specification use explicit end tags.


<h2 id=the-json-attribute>The <code>json</code> attribute</h2>

<h3 id=json-content-attribute>The content attribute</h3>

The <dfn element-attr for=template>json</dfn> content attribute can be specified on <{template}>
elements. If present, its value must be a [=valid non-empty URL potentially surrounded by spaces=]
that identifies a JSON resource.

Each <{template}> element has a <dfn for=template>JSON data</dfn>, which is either null or a
[=list=]. It is initially null.

Each <{template}> element has a <dfn for=template>JSON fetch controller</dfn>, which is either null
or a [=fetch controller=]. It is initially null.

<h3 id=json-idl-attribute>The IDL attribute</h3>

<xmp class=idl>
partial interface HTMLTemplateElement {
  [CEReactions, ReflectURL] attribute USVString json;
};
</xmp>

The <dfn attribute for=HTMLTemplateElement>json</dfn> IDL attribute must [=reflect=] the
<{template/json}> content attribute, as a URL.

<h3 id=loading-json-data>Loading the data</h3>

When a <{template}> element [=becomes connected=] or becomes disconnected, and when its
<{template/json}> attribute is set, changed, or removed, the user agent must [=update the JSON
data=] of the element.

<div algorithm>
    To <dfn export>update the JSON data</dfn> of a <{template}> element |template|:

    1. If |template|'s [=template/JSON fetch controller=] is not null, then [=fetch controller/abort=]
        it, and set |template|'s [=template/JSON fetch controller=] to null.

    1. Set |template|'s [=template/JSON data=] to null.

    1. If |template| is not [=connected=], or it does not have a <{template/json}> attribute, then
        return.

    1. Let |url| be the result of [=encoding-parsing a URL=] given the value of |template|'s
        <{template/json}> attribute, relative to |template|'s [=Node/node document=].

    1. If |url| is failure, then [=queue an element task=] on the [=DOM manipulation task source=]
        given |template| to [=fire an event=] named <code>error</code> at |template|, and return.

    1. Let |request| be a new [=request=] whose [=request/URL=] is |url|, [=request/client=] is
        |template|'s [=Node/node document=]'s [=relevant settings object=], [=request/destination=]
        is "<code>json</code>", [=request/mode=] is "<code>cors</code>", and
        [=request/credentials mode=] is "<code>same-origin</code>".

    1. Set |template|'s [=template/JSON fetch controller=] to the result of [=fetching=] |request|,
        with <i>[=fetch/processResponseConsumeBody=]</i> set to the following steps given a
        [=response=] |response| and null, failure, or a [=byte sequence=] |bodyBytes|:

        1. Set |template|'s [=template/JSON fetch controller=] to null.

        1. If |response| is a [=network error=], or its [=response/status=] is not an
            [=ok status=], or |bodyBytes| is null or failure, then [=fire an event=] named
            <code>error</code> at |template|, and return.

        1. Let |value| be the result of [=parse JSON bytes to an Infra value|parsing JSON bytes to
            an Infra value=] given |bodyBytes|. If this throws an exception, then [=fire an event=]
            named <code>error</code> at |template|, and return.

        1. If |value| is a [=list=], then set |template|'s [=template/JSON data=] to |value|.
            Otherwise, set it to « |value| ».

        1. [=list/For each=] |host| of |template|'s [=template hosts=], [=render a template|render=]
            |host|.

        1. [=Fire an event=] named <code>load</code> at |template|.
</div>

Note: A template loads its data once, however many elements render it. Moving a template to another
place in the document loads the data again.


<h2 id=the-template-attribute>The <code>template</code> attribute</h2>

<h3 id=template-content-attribute>The content attribute</h3>

The <dfn element-attr for=html-global>template</dfn> content attribute can be specified on any
[=HTML element=]. If present, its value must be the [=Element/ID=] of a <{template}> element in the
same [=tree=].

The <dfn>referenced template</dfn> of an [=element=] |host| is found by these steps:

1. If |host| does not have a <{html-global/template}> attribute, or its value is the empty string,
    then return null.

1. Let |candidate| be the first [=element=], in [=tree order=], among the [=descendants=] of
    |host|'s [=tree/root=] whose [=Element/ID=] is the value of |host|'s <{html-global/template}>
    attribute.

1. If |candidate| is a <{template}> element, then return |candidate|. Otherwise, return null.

The <dfn>template hosts</dfn> of a <{template}> element |template| are the [=elements=], in
[=tree order=], among the [=descendants=] of |template|'s [=tree/root=] whose
[=referenced template=] is |template|.

<h3 id=template-idl-attribute>The IDL attribute</h3>

<xmp class=idl>
partial interface HTMLElement {
  [CEReactions, Reflect] attribute DOMString template;
};
</xmp>

The <dfn attribute for=HTMLElement>template</dfn> IDL attribute must [=reflect=] the
<{html-global/template}> content attribute.


<h2 id=the-key-attribute>The <code>key</code> attribute</h2>

<h3 id=key-content-attribute>The content attribute</h3>

The <dfn element-attr for=html-global>key</dfn> content attribute can be specified on any
[=HTML element=]. Its value is any string. It marks an element in a template's
[=template contents=] as the place where the item property with the same name is bound. Outside a
template's contents, and in copies that have already been rendered, the attribute has no effect.

Unlike IDs, keys don't need to be unique. Every element in a copy whose key matches a property
receives that property's value.

<h3 id=key-idl-attribute>The IDL attribute</h3>

<xmp class=idl>
partial interface HTMLElement {
  [CEReactions, Reflect] attribute DOMString key;
};
</xmp>

The <dfn attribute for=HTMLElement>key</dfn> IDL attribute must [=reflect=] the
<{html-global/key}> content attribute.

<div class=example id=example-idl>
    <xmp highlight=js>
    const template = document.getElementById("tableRow");
    const tbody = document.querySelector("tbody");

    template.json;                              // "https://example.com/json/tableData.json"
    tbody.template;                             // "tableRow"
    template.content.querySelector("td").key;   // "city"

    template.addEventListener("load", () => console.log("rows rendered"));
    template.addEventListener("error", () => console.log("data failed to load"));

    // Feature detection.
    const supported = "json" in HTMLTemplateElement.prototype;
    </xmp>
</div>


<h2 id=rendering>Rendering</h2>

<h3 id=when-to-render>When to render</h3>

The user agent must [=queue an element task=] on the [=DOM manipulation task source=] given an
[=element=] |host| to [=render a template|render=] |host| when either of these happens:

* |host| [=becomes connected=] while it has a <{html-global/template}> attribute; or
* |host|'s <{html-global/template}> attribute is set or changed while |host| is [=connected=].

When a <{template}> element's <code>id</code> attribute is set, changed, or removed while the
element is [=connected=], the user agent must [=queue an element task=] on the
[=DOM manipulation task source=] given the element to [=render a template|render=] each of its
[=template hosts=].

<h3 id=rendering-a-template>Rendering a template</h3>

<div algorithm>
    To <dfn export>render a template</dfn> into an [=element=] |host|:

    1. Let |template| be |host|'s [=referenced template=].

    1. If |template| is null, or |template|'s [=template/JSON data=] is null, then return.

    1. If |host| has an [=ancestor=] whose <{html-global/template}> attribute has the same value as
        |host|'s <{html-global/template}> attribute, then return.

    1. Let |fragment| be a new {{DocumentFragment}} whose [=Node/node document=] is |host|'s
        [=Node/node document=].

    1. [=list/For each=] |item| of |template|'s [=template/JSON data=]:

        1. If |item| is not an [=ordered map=], then [=iteration/continue=].

        1. Let |clone| be the result of [=clone a node|cloning=] |template|'s [=template contents=],
            with <i>document</i> set to |host|'s [=Node/node document=] and <i>subtree</i> set to
            true.

        1. Let |keyed| be a [=list=] of the [=elements=] among the [=descendants=] of |clone| that
            have a <{html-global/key}> attribute, in [=tree order=].

        1. [=list/For each=] |element| of |keyed|:

            1. Let |key| be the value of |element|'s <{html-global/key}> attribute.

            1. If |item|[|key|] [=map/exists=], then [=bind a value=] given |element| and
                |item|[|key|].

        1. [=Append=] |clone| to |fragment|.

    1. [=Node/Replace all=] with |fragment| within |host|.
</div>

Note: The ancestor check stops a template that contains its own host from rendering forever. A
template can contain hosts for other templates, and those render normally.

<h3 id=binding-values>Binding values</h3>

<div algorithm>
    To <dfn>bind a value</dfn> given an [=element=] |element| and an Infra value |value|:

    1. If |element| is a <{script}> element, or an [=element=] whose [=Element/local name=] is
        <code>script</code> and whose [=Element/namespace=] is the [=SVG namespace=], then return.

    1. If |value| is not an [=ordered map=], then [=bind text=] given |element| and |value|, and
        return.

    1. [=map/For each=] |name| → |propertyValue| of |value|:

        1. If |name| is "<code>content</code>", then [=bind text=] given |element| and
            |propertyValue|.

        1. Otherwise, [=bind an attribute=] given |element|, |name|, and |propertyValue|.
</div>

<div algorithm>
    To <dfn>bind text</dfn> given an [=element=] |element| and an Infra value |value|:

    1. If |value| is a [=string=], then [=string replace all=] with |value| within |element|.

    1. Otherwise, if |value| is a number, then [=string replace all=] with the result of
        <a abstract-op>ToString</a>(|value|) within |element|.

    1. Otherwise, if |value| is true or false, then [=string replace all=] with "<code>true</code>"
        or "<code>false</code>" within |element|.

    1. Otherwise, if |value| is null, then [=string replace all=] with the empty string within
        |element|.

    1. Otherwise, |value| is a [=list=] or an [=ordered map=]. Do nothing.
</div>

<div algorithm>
    To <dfn>bind an attribute</dfn> given an [=element=] |element|, a [=string=] |name|, and an
    Infra value |value|:

    1. If |name| is not a [=valid attribute local name=], then return.

    1. If |element| is an [=HTML element=] and its [=Node/node document=] is an [=HTML document=],
        then set |name| to |name|, [=ASCII lowercased=].

    1. If |name| [=string/starts with=] "<code>on</code>" or is "<code>srcdoc</code>", then return.

    1. If |value| is false or null, then [=remove an attribute by name=] given |name| and |element|,
        and return.

    1. If |value| is a [=list=] or an [=ordered map=], then return.

    1. Let |text| be |value| if |value| is a [=string=], the empty string if |value| is true, or the
        result of <a abstract-op>ToString</a>(|value|) if |value| is a number.

    1. If the result of running the [=basic URL parser=] on |text| is not failure, and its
        [=url/scheme=] is "<code>javascript</code>", then return.

    1. [=Set an attribute value=] given |element|, |name|, and |text|.
</div>

<div class=example id=example-binding-table>
    How each value binds into <code>&lt;input key="field"></code>:

    <table class=data>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Value of <code>field</code><th>Result
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>"Seattle"</code><td>Text content <code>Seattle</code>
            <tr><td><code>{ "value": "Seattle" }</code><td><code>value="Seattle"</code>
            <tr><td><code>{ "maxlength": 3 }</code><td><code>maxlength="3"</code>
            <tr><td><code>{ "required": true }</code><td><code>required=""</code>
            <tr><td><code>{ "required": false }</code> or <code>{ "required": null }</code><td>No
                <code>required</code> attribute
            <tr><td><code>{ "aria-invalid": "false" }</code><td><code>aria-invalid="false"</code>
            <tr><td><code>{ "onclick": "…" }</code><td>Ignored
            <tr><td><code>{ "formaction": "javascript:…" }</code><td>Ignored
    </table>
</div>

Note: <code>true</code> and <code>false</code> map to the presence and absence of an attribute, which
suits boolean attributes such as <code>required</code>, <code>checked</code>, and
<code>selected</code>. Attributes that take the strings <code>"true"</code> and <code>"false"</code>,
such as <code>aria-invalid</code>, need string values.

Note: Values are inserted as text. A value such as <code>"&lt;b>Seattle&lt;/b>"</code> appears as
those characters, and is never parsed as markup.


<h2 id=other-features>Interaction with other features</h2>

*This section is non-normative.*

: Fallback content
:: The host's original children stay in place until the data loads. They also stay if the data
    fails to load, and in user agents without support.
: Keys in the copies
:: Copies keep their <{html-global/key}> attributes, so authors can select and style them, for
    example with <code>[key="city"]</code>.
: IDs in the template
:: <code>id</code> attributes are copied unchanged. A template rendered more than once should not
    contain IDs, or the page will have duplicates.
: Missing properties
:: If an item has no property for a key, that element keeps whatever the template gave it, such as
    placeholder text or default attributes.
: Keyed elements inside keyed elements
:: Binding <code>content</code> to an element replaces its children, including any keyed elements
    inside it.
: Several hosts
:: Any number of elements can name the same template. They share one request, and each gets its own
    copies.
: Order in the document
:: A host can come before or after its template. Hosts render when the data arrives, and hosts added
    later render as soon as they are connected.
: Tables and selects
:: <{template}> elements are allowed inside <{table}> and <{select}> elements, and the parser keeps
    <code>tr</code>, <code>td</code>, and <code>option</code> elements in a template's contents, so
    rows and options can be templated directly.
: Form controls
:: Copies are new elements, so binding <code>value</code>, <code>checked</code>, or
    <code>selected</code> sets what the control shows.
: Custom elements
:: Custom elements in the copies are upgraded when the copies are inserted, as with any other
    inserted content.
: Script
:: The <code>load</code> and <code>error</code> events on the template tell scripts when rendering
    finishes or fails. After rendering, the copies are ordinary DOM that scripts can change.


<h2 id=changes-to-html>Changes to the HTML Standard</h2>

To integrate this specification into the HTML Standard [[!HTML]], make these changes:

1. In <a href="https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/scripting.html#the-template-element">the
    <code>template</code> element</a>:
    * Add <{template/json}> to the element's content attributes.
    * Add the <code>json</code> IDL attribute to the {{HTMLTemplateElement}} interface.
    * Add the [=template/JSON data=] and [=template/JSON fetch controller=] concepts, and the
        [=update the JSON data=] algorithm and the conditions that run it.
1. In <a href="https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/dom.html#global-attributes">global
    attributes</a>, add <{html-global/template}> and <{html-global/key}>, and add the
    <code>template</code> and <code>key</code> IDL attributes to the {{HTMLElement}} interface.
1. Add [[#template-content-attribute]], [[#key-content-attribute]], and [[#rendering]] as a new
    subsection after the <code>template</code> element.
1. In the index of element attributes, add these rows:
    * attribute <code>json</code>; element <code>template</code>; description "JSON data to render
        the template with"; value "Valid non-empty URL potentially surrounded by spaces".
    * attribute <code>template</code>; elements "HTML elements"; description "Template to render
        into the element"; value "ID of a <code>template</code> element".
    * attribute <code>key</code>; elements "HTML elements"; description "Name of the data property
        bound to the element"; value "Text".
1. In the index of events, add <code>load</code> and <code>error</code> as fired at
    <code>template</code> elements.


<h2 id=a11y>Accessibility considerations</h2>

Rendered content is ordinary DOM, so assistive technologies see the same table rows, list items, or
options that any other page would have.

Because attributes can be bound, data can and should supply accessible names and descriptions, such
as <code>alt</code> for images and <code>aria-label</code> for icon-only links. Templates should
give these attributes a sensible default in case the data leaves them out.

Rendering replaces the host's contents after the page has loaded, and assistive technologies might
not announce the change. Authors can set <code>aria-busy="true"</code> on the host and remove it in
a <code>load</code> event listener, or describe the loading state in the fallback content.

Fallback content should make sense on its own, because some users will only ever see it.


<h2 id=security-privacy>Security and privacy considerations</h2>

JSON data can come from a different party than the page, such as a third-party API or content that
users submitted. Binding is designed so that data can fill in a template but cannot add behavior to
it:

* Text values are inserted as text and never parsed as markup.
* Data can't create elements. It can only change elements the author put in the template.
* Event handler attributes, whose names start with <code>on</code>, are ignored.
* <code>srcdoc</code> is ignored, because it would put markup from the data into an
    <{iframe}> with the page's origin.
* Values that are <code>javascript:</code> URLs are ignored for every attribute, so data can't
    turn a link, form action, or frame into script.
* <{script}> elements, in HTML and SVG, never receive values, so data can't set a script's source
    or text.

Some risks remain, and authors should consider them before binding data they don't control:

* A bound <code>style</code> attribute, or text bound into a <code>style</code> element, can change
    how the page looks, and CSS can be used to leak information.
* A bound <code>href</code> or <code>src</code> can point anywhere other than a
    <code>javascript:</code> URL, including tracking pixels and phishing pages.

Loading the data:

* The request uses CORS. A cross-origin resource must opt in with
    <code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin</code>, and credentials are sent only to the page's own
    origin. The page learns nothing that <code>fetch()</code> couldn't already tell it.
* The request happens without script, like an image or a stylesheet. It reveals no more about the
    user than those requests do.
* The request is subject to Content Security Policy like any other fetch. See
    [[#open-issues]] for which directive applies.


<h2 id=more-examples>More examples</h2>

*This section is non-normative.*

<div class=example id=example-list>
    A team list with links and photos, and a fallback link for user agents without support.

    <xmp highlight=html>
    <template id="person" json="/team.json">
      <li>
        <img key="photo" src="/avatar.png" alt="">
        <a key="name"></a>, <span key="role"></span>
      </li>
    </template>

    <ul template="person">
      <li><a href="/team.json">View the team</a></li>
    </ul>
    </xmp>

    <xmp highlight=json>
    [
      {
        "photo": { "src": "/ana.jpg", "alt": "Ana" },
        "name": { "href": "/team/ana", "content": "Ana" },
        "role": "Designer"
      }
    ]
    </xmp>

    The list becomes:

    <xmp highlight=html>
    <li>
      <img key="photo" src="/ana.jpg" alt="Ana">
      <a key="name" href="/team/ana">Ana</a>, <span key="role">Designer</span>
    </li>
    </xmp>
</div>

<div class=example id=example-select>
    Options for a <{select}> element, with a value that differs from the label, and one option
    selected.

    <xmp highlight=html>
    <template id="stateOption" json="/states.json">
      <option key="state"></option>
    </template>

    <label>State
      <select name="state" template="stateOption">
        <option>Loading…</option>
      </select>
    </label>
    </xmp>

    <xmp highlight=json>
    [
      { "state": { "value": "OR", "content": "Oregon" } },
      { "state": { "value": "WA", "content": "Washington", "selected": true } }
    ]
    </xmp>
</div>

<div class=example id=example-shared>
    Two hosts share one template, and so one request. A single object, rather than an array, renders
    once.

    <xmp highlight=html>
    <template id="summary" json="/stats.json">
      <span key="count"></span> cities
    </template>

    <p template="summary">…</p>
    <footer template="summary">…</footer>
    </xmp>

    With <code>{"count": 3}</code>, both elements show "3 cities".
</div>


<h2 id=alternate-proposals>Alternate proposals</h2>

*This section is non-normative.*

<h3 id=alt-array-of-bindings>Array of bindings (rejected)</h3>

**Status: rejected.** Each item would be an array of binding objects, each naming its element with a
<code>key</code> property, instead of a map from keys to values:

<xmp highlight=json>
[
  [
    { "key": "city",  "content": "Seattle" },
    { "key": "link",  "href": "/wa/seattle", "content": "Details" },
    { "key": "photo", "src": "seattle.jpg", "alt": "Seattle skyline" }
  ],
  [
    { "key": "city",  "content": "Portland" },
    { "key": "link",  "href": "/or/portland", "content": "Details" },
    { "key": "photo", "src": "portland.jpg", "alt": "Portland bridges" }
  ]
]
</xmp>

It renders the same rows as the <a href="#example-intro-attributes">second introduction example</a>, but it was rejected because:

* Every value needs a wrapper object, even plain text, so the data is longer.
* The same key can appear twice in one row, so the specification would need a rule for which
    binding wins.
* <code>key</code> becomes a reserved property name, so data couldn't set a <code>key</code>
    attribute.
* Data from APIs and spreadsheets is usually already a list of objects, so it would need converting
    first.

<h3 id=alt-script>Script</h3>

Authors can already fetch the JSON, clone the template, and fill in the copies with script. It
takes a dozen lines, has to be repeated or packaged for each page, and shows nothing when script is
turned off or fails.

<h3 id=alt-template-instantiation>Template instantiation and DOM Parts</h3>

The Web Components community has discussed template instantiation and DOM Parts: script APIs that
mark the parts of a template that change and update them efficiently. Those proposals serve
frameworks that update the page many times. This specification covers the simpler case of rendering
static data once, without script, and could later be built on top of DOM Parts.

<h3 id=alt-src>A <code>src</code> attribute</h3>

The template could use <code>src</code> instead of <code>json</code>. The name <code>json</code>
makes the expected format clear, and leaves room for other formats later.


<h2 id=open-issues>Open issues</h2>

Issue: <strong>The <code>content</code> property.</strong> Because <code>content</code> means the
element's text, data can't set a <code>content</code> attribute, such as the one on
<code>meta</code> elements. Should the text use a name that can't be an attribute, such as
<code>#text</code>?

Issue: <strong>Attribute guards.</strong> Is a fixed list of ignored attributes and values enough,
or should binding integrate with Trusted Types and the HTML Sanitizer API's configuration?

Issue: <strong>Nested data.</strong> A property whose value is an array is ignored. Should a host
inside a template render that array, for example the list of cities inside each state?

Issue: <strong>Part of the resource.</strong> Should authors be able to render part of the JSON,
such as the <code>cities</code> property of the top-level object?

Issue: <strong>Refreshing and script-supplied data.</strong> Should there be a way to load the data
again, such as a <code>reload()</code> method, or to give a template data from script?

Issue: <strong>Load event.</strong> Should loading the data delay the document's <code>load</code>
event, as images do?

Issue: <strong>Content Security Policy.</strong> Which directive governs these requests?
<code>connect-src</code> matches <code>fetch()</code>.

Issue: <strong>Attribute names.</strong> Are <code>template</code> and <code>key</code> safe as new
global attributes and {{HTMLElement}} properties, given that some libraries and custom elements
already use properties with those names?
